```js
const API_KEY = process.env.EXAMPLE_API_KEY;

async function getUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${id}`, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${API_KEY}` },
  });
  if (!res.ok) {
    throw new Error(`GET /users/${id} failed: ${res.status}`);
  }
  const data = await res.json();
  return data.user;
}
```

I made two behavior changes. I added a check on `res.ok`, because `fetch` only rejects on network errors: a 404 or 500 would otherwise be parsed as if it were a valid user. And I let errors propagate instead of catching them here, since your `.catch` logged the error and then returned `undefined`, which callers couldn't tell apart from a user with no data. Put the `try/catch` where you actually handle the failure.

Your original code has a live secret key hard-coded. If it's a real key, rotate it in the provider's dashboard and read the new one from an environment variable as above. If this code runs in the browser rather than in Node, `process.env` won't exist and no secret key is safe there at all: the request has to go through your backend.

