È il comportamento di `space-between`: distribuisce lo spazio riga per riga, quindi l'ultima riga con due elementi li spinge ai lati. Per una griglia di elementi uguali usa grid, che allinea tutte le righe alle stesse colonne:

```css
.galleria {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
```

Le colonne sono larghe almeno 220px e se ne mettono quante ne stanno, e l'ultima riga riempie le colonne da sinistra come le altre. Lo spazio tra gli elementi lo dà `gap`, quindi puoi togliere `justify-content` e gli eventuali margini sulle card.

Allora dai alle colonne una larghezza fissa e centra la griglia nel contenitore:

```css
.galleria {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, 250px);
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
```

Qui `justify-content: center` centra l'insieme delle colonne, non i singoli elementi di ogni riga, quindi l'ultima riga resta allineata a sinistra sotto le altre invece di centrarsi da sola.

Una cosa da controllare: su uno schermo più stretto di 250px più i margini della pagina la colonna non ci sta. Con `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(250px, 100%), 250px))` la card si stringe solo in quel caso.

Define the brand once in OKLCH and derive the states by changing only the lightness. OKLCH lightness is perceptually even, so the same step looks like the same amount of darkening on any hue, which is not true of HSL (a yellow and a blue at the same HSL lightness look nothing alike).

With relative color syntax:

```css
:root {
  --brand: oklch(0.55 0.15 255);                               /* #2971c6 */
  --brand-hover: oklch(from var(--brand) calc(l - 0.07) c h);  /* #0e5caf */
  --brand-active: oklch(from var(--brand) calc(l - 0.12) c h);
  --brand-subtle: oklch(from var(--brand) 0.95 0.03 h);        /* tinted background */
}
```

Change `--brand` and every state follows. Relative color syntax works in current Chrome, Edge, Safari and Firefox, but only in fairly recent versions. If you need older browsers, `color-mix()` has been supported longer and gets close enough:

```css
:root {
  --brand-hover: color-mix(in oklch, var(--brand), black 12%);
  --brand-subtle: color-mix(in oklch, var(--brand), white 88%);
}
```

Two things to keep checking:

- **Contrast.** White on the base color above is about 4.9:1, which passes for normal text; the darker states only improve it. If you pick a lighter brand, the base may fail even when the hover passes.
- **Gamut.** A saturated color pushed darker or lighter can fall outside sRGB (the active state here does, slightly). Browsers map it back automatically, but the result may shift a little, so look at the states on screen rather than trusting the numbers alone. Lowering chroma a bit for the extreme steps fixes it.

why does this print 4 4 4 instead of 1 2 3

for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), i * 1000);
}

`var` creates a single `i` for the whole function, not one per iteration. All three callbacks close over that same variable, and by the time they run the loop has finished and `i` is 4.

Change `var` to `let`:

```js
for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), i * 1000);
}
// 1, 2, 3, one per second
```

With `let`, a `for` loop creates a fresh binding of `i` for each iteration, so each callback captures its own value. The delays were already right in your version because `i * 1000` is evaluated immediately; only the callbacks read `i` later.

